	"EngineVersion": "5.8.0",
	"FriendlyName": "BeltLine - Conveyor & Item Logistics on a Budget",
	"Description": "Conveyor belts along splines carrying thousands of items, without an actor per item and without a tick per item. An item is an entry in a ring buffer on its belt - a distance and a type index, sixteen bytes, no UObject - and everything of one type is drawn as a single instanced set, so ten thousand crates cost one draw call per item type rather than ten thousand components. One belt update is one pass over a flat float array; the transform for each item comes from a baked path table, not from a spline evaluation, so the cost per item is two array lookups and a lerp. Backpressure is part of the model rather than an afterthought: block the output and the queue grows backwards at minimum spacing, items neither overlap nor vanish, the throughput counter falls to zero, and releasing the block lets the queue drain again. Belt nodes hand items between belts with a capacity and an optional per-type filter, which is enough for branching and merging. A global item budget caps the world's item count; lowering it removes items without freeing an instance slot, so the draw call count does not move and nothing is reallocated. Every number the plugin claims - items, instance sets, throughput per second, jammed items, milliseconds per update, buffer growth events - is on a Canvas statistics box that survives a Shipping build. BeltLine is not a factory game: it ships no recipes, no crafting, no machines, no inventory, no physics on the items and no network replication.",
	"Category": "Gameplay",
	"CreatedBy": "Silvan Teufel",
	"CreatedByURL": "https://silvan.teufel-engineering.com",
